The OLED has a layered structure comprising:

- a substrate;
- a first electrode, typically a transparent anode;
- one or more conductive current-spreading elements, such as metal bus lines or a grid, electrically connected to the first electrode to reduce lateral voltage drop;
- a passivation coating covering the surfaces of each current-spreading element that face the active OLED stack, the coating comprising a metal sulfide, selenide, or telluride;
- organic charge-transport and light-emitting layers deposited over the first electrode and the coated spreading elements; and
- a second electrode, typically the cathode, over the organic layers.

The chalcogenide coating separates the conductive spreading elements from the organic layers, preventing direct electrical contact, short circuits, and adverse chemical interaction, while the elements remain connected to the underlying electrode for current distribution.

1. **An organic light-emitting component comprising:**

a substrate;

a first electrode disposed over the substrate;

an organic radiation-emitting layer stack disposed over the first electrode;

a second electrode disposed over the organic radiation-emitting layer stack, such that the organic radiation-emitting layer stack is arranged between the first electrode and the second electrode; and

a current-spreading structure comprising a metal electrically coupled to at least one of the first electrode and the second electrode and configured to distribute electrical current across the organic radiation-emitting layer stack, at least a portion of the metal being covered by a passivation material that inhibits interaction of the metal with the organic radiation-emitting layer stack or an ambient environment.
